cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Divergence Ranks What Benchmarks Bound: quantization, context and speculative decoding for a 27B coding model on two 16 GB GPUs"
abstract: >-
  Quantization quality for locally served models is published on prose corpora and
  certified on task benchmarks. We measure a 27B coding model across four llama.cpp
  GGUF quantizations on two consumer 16 GB GPUs, and find that the instrument decides
  whether there is anything to see. KL divergence against the least-quantized
  available arm, over 65,536 tokens per domain, separates every adjacent pair on code
  at 8.7-18.1 sigma and is roughly twice as large on code as on prose, rising again to
  3.1-4.4x prose on the benchmark's own prompts. Three task benchmarks -- multiple
  choice, unit-tested generation, and long-context retrieval -- bound the whole
  ladder's effect at about three points of pass@1 and cannot resolve below it; for two
  of them, no outcome could have reached significance at the discordance rates
  observed. The same checkpoint's reported degradation moves 36-fold under two
  defensible perplexity protocols, from 29 per cent worse to 0.8 per cent worse,
  attributable to corpus file, window coverage and scoring rule alone. The divergence
  distribution explains what the benchmarks miss: on this code corpus the median token
  is perturbed 181-206x less than on prose while the top percentile is perturbed
  5.0-8.1x more -- a shape we show is set by the corpus rather than by the
  compression, since changing only the KV-cache dtype reproduces it. We also report
  that GPU tensor placement rather than quantization sets the reachable context window
  on this host, and that speculative decoding is deterministically non-equivalent to
  unspeculated decoding. Artifacts and the full measurement record are released.
type: dataset
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/henrique-simoes/multivac-paper"
keywords:
  - quantization
  - large language models
  - llama.cpp
  - GGUF
  - KL divergence
  - long context
  - speculative decoding
  - benchmarking
